    "q-156596": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156596",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=-x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $y=-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\ (0) & =-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=-x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
