    "q-156624": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156624",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=2y-1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x=2y-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(1)-1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
